  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/b4/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_119.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Dr. Strange is up late studying a strange gem, fighting to stay awake, when two unfortunate burglars prove themselves dumb enough to climb into his living quarters through an open window!&nbsp; He is annoyed, but decides he can't be bothered to do anything other than cast them out of his house without even summoning the police, which makes the crooks think \"he must be on the lam.\"&nbsp; Believing that this will prevent the owner from reporting their crime to the police, they feel emboldened enough to return the next evening!\n\nAfter giving his servant, Wong, the rest of the night off, Dr. Strange himself plans to spend the evening in solitude and meditation, not realizing that the two crooks have returned.&nbsp; They plan to rob the house while Strange is tucked away in his study, stumbling across the beautiful gem almost at once.&nbsp; Without hesitation, they grab it and count their blessings, believing it to be \"worth a cool <i>million</i>!\"\n\nBut their handling of the gem only alerts Strange to a terrible danger, and he <i>immediately</i> sends out his \"ethereal form\" to investigate!&nbsp; Finding the gem gone, he wonders who would be foolish enough to \"take so dangerous an object,\" finally using his amulet to help track the crooks to their hideout.&nbsp; The poor fools have no idea that the gem serves as a bridge between dimensions, in this case, the \"dread Purple Dimension!\"&nbsp; By the time Strange returns in his physical form, he finds only a purple mist -- he is too late to save them!\n\nEven though they are crooks, he has to save them by going to this Purple Dimension himself!&nbsp; There he finds the realm's overlord (later identified as \"Aggamon\"), who is making slaves of the two men (and many others).&nbsp; All who enter are forced to serve him, and he points out that no one has escaped in a thousand years!\n\nBut Strange defies the overlord, claiming that his power is <i>equal</i> to his own.&nbsp; The very notion seems absurd to Aggamon, but he senses that having a captive as powerful as Dr. Strange would prove to be an advantage, and he offers to return the two crooks to their \"natural world\" if Strange will agree to stay in their place.&nbsp; Strange isn't happy about having to make such a sacrifice for two who are \"unworthy,\" but he agrees to the terms.\n\nAs soon as the pair of criminals are returned to Earth, Strange points out to his host that, though he agreed to <i>stay</i>, he nevertheless did not agree to <i>remain</i>!&nbsp; And with that, Dr. Strange calls on his \"awesome amulet,\" and in seconds, the battle commences!\n\nThe various guards are scattered by the power of the visiting human, but Aggamon himself whips out his \"jeweled demolisher beam\" and fires it.&nbsp; At the same time, Dr. Strange counters with his mystical amulet.&nbsp; But the beams seem equally matched, and \"as the hours creep by,\" both feel their life essences draining away.&nbsp; The overlord, determined to survive, agrees to surrender.&nbsp; But Doctor Strange uses his amulet to further weaken the overlord and force him to set <i>all</i> his captives free.\n\nWhen Strange returns to Earth, he is informed by a policeman that the two crooks were so scared by their experience, they turned <i>themselves</i> in.&nbsp; Strange thought he was risking his life in vain, but he is happy to hear that \"because of my struggle, two humans have been put upon the right path!\"&nbsp; He decides to keep the strange gem, just in case he's needed beyond the purple veil again.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Two bumbling crooks steal a mysterious gem and inadvertently stumble into the Purple Dimension but sadly can't open the door to a more engaging story.",
  "subtitle": "Another Day, Another Dimension",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | NotWorthMyTime | width=13%] A readable tale, but only just.\n[BR]\n[BR] This is perhaps better than earlier offerings, but let's face it, the stakes are [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<i>very</i> low here] -- only two mundane crooks' lives are at risk -- two who are dumb enough to believe that, just because Strange simply shrugs them off at first, he isn't interested in dealing with the police!\n\nI admit that it's gratifying to learn Dr. Strange considers all people worthy of his efforts, be they criminal or otherwise, even if he initially considers them beneath his notice, [SPAN: severity=error; text=but the story has <i>no interest</i> in the burglary itself.]&nbsp; It only wants an excuse to propel Strange into yet <i>another</i> mystical realm, identified this time as \"the dread <i>Purple Dimension</i>.\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=(Collective gasp!)]\n[ASSET: center | PoorFools | width=67%]\n\nThe plot not only doesn't actually care about these two crooks, [SPAN: severity=error; text=it can't even be bothered to explain the origin of this mysterious gem... not so much as a <i>hint</i> of where it came from.]\n\nNo... details like that seem beneath the notice of both writer and artist, who are only interested in ushering the reader to the <i>horrors</i> of... the <b>Purple Dimension!</b>  [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Oh no!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | AggamonOverlord | width=18%] What do we ultimately find in this \"dread\" place?\n[BR]\n[BR] Only that it has a bunch of slaves who have been captured from other realms and sent to work in the mines for the overlord, Aggamon, all for one purpose:&nbsp; to \"delight my soul!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] When Dr. Strange objects, one has to wonder why Aggamon even <i>wants</i> someone so powerful.&nbsp; Everything we've been shown suggests that simple physical labor is all he <i>really</i> needs.\n\n[HANG0] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Once more, the story doesn't seem interested in actually making sense.]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=It's only determined to stage a duel!]\n\nAfter dispensing with the two criminals by returning them to Earth, the true conflict begins, and the net result is little more than an hours-long battle that amounts to two powerful beams aimed at each other, both combatants simply standing there, waiting for the other to concede.\n[ASSET: center | EquallyMatched | width=67%]\n\nThe entire duel is over in a single page, and... [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold-italic; text=surprise, surprise...]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Can you guess who wins?)]\n\n[ASSET: left | TurnThemselvesIn | width=14%] Aggamon concedes defeat, all his captives are freed, Strange returns to Earth, and in a final act of [SPAN: severity=error; text=gag-inducing tidiness], the two crooks are shown to have turned themselves into police custody.\n[BR]\n[BR] Strange walks away, happy for the small role he played in helping to set two seemingly-unworthy humans on the \"right path.\"\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | WongNamed | width=21%] Along the way, Stan and Steve finally reveal the name of the character who answered the door on page 2 of [LINK:1963:ST_110_strange2|ST #110strange|2026-09-20], where Doctor Strange made his own first appearance.&nbsp; In this story, the servant is identified as \"Wong\" and given the night off.&nbsp; (Wong had also appeared in a single panel of [LINK:1963:ST_116_strange2|ST #116strange|2027-02-22].)\n[BR]\n[BR] His very brief appearances only highlight to readers how very disconnected Dr. Strange seems to be from the world at large.&nbsp; In this case, it's not made clear whether Wong lives elsewhere, or inside the home of his master.&nbsp; He is only \"dismissed!\"\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt's a shame that reading the adventures of Doctor Strange amounts to little more than [SPAN: severity=info; text=mystical this, that, or the other thing], routinely substituting spectacle for any actual substance.\n\nThe moment a scene threatens to involve mundane matters such as a home robbery (things that an average reader can actually <i>understand</i>), the plot races at breakneck speed to move away from such things and rush forward into swirling dimensions, hand waving, spells, and in this case, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=beams of light.]\n\nDoctor Strange may own his own home and even have a servant (who we now know is named Wong), but <i>geez</i>, [SPAN: severity=error; text=he can't even be bothered to close his windows in the evening to keep riff-raff from climbing in!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=This is a character who shows absolutely no interest in the things of Earth.]\n[BR] He seems as disconnected from the rest of the Marvel Universe as it is possible to imagine!\n\nEven after a half-dozen stories, I find that there's still nothing here that makes a reader want to <i>care</i> about this character, and the plots largely feel like they're just going through the motions.\n\nIn the end, I liked the two throwaway crooks more than I liked Doctor Strange.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=At least <i>they</i> went straight!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=As for the title character?&nbsp; <b>He seems stuck in neutral.</b>]",
  "highlight": "Doctor Strange tracks two burglars into the Purple Dimension, confronts its overlord, and wins a mystical beam battle that never quite comes to life."
}